   "Corporate concentration in the US has risen steadily since the 1930s, with the sales share of the top 1% of firms increasing from ~60% in the 1960s-70s to ~80% recently.",
   "The most compelling hypothesis for rising concentration is that new technologies enable a small number of firms to leverage economies of scale and network effects, capturing larger market shares.",
   "Rising concentration accounts for approximately one-third of the increase in US corporate profit margins since 2000.",
   "Industries most exposed to AI (e.g., information, finance) are currently more concentrated and operate with higher margins; while AI could foster competition, historical tech shocks suggest it may further increase concentration as leading firms capture scale and network effects.",
